# Part 2 — Docker Container Management

> Run these commands in Windows PowerShell or CMD, unless otherwise specified.

## 9. Check Docker Installation and Status

```powershell
# Display the Docker CLI version
docker --version

# Display client and server versions
docker version

# Display Docker Engine information
docker info

# Check Docker Compose
docker compose version
```

`docker info` also helps determine whether the Docker Engine is reachable.

## 10. List Containers

```powershell
# List running containers
docker ps

# List all containers, including stopped ones
docker ps -a

# Display only running container IDs
docker ps -q
```

## 11. Start, Stop, and Restart Containers

```powershell
# Start a stopped container
docker start debian-lab

# Stop a running container
docker stop debian-lab

# Restart a container
docker restart debian-lab
```

Stopping a container does not normally delete its writable layer.

## 12. Access a Container

```powershell
# Create and start a Debian container interactively
docker run -it --name debian-lab debian:bookworm bash

# Start the existing container and attach to its main process
docker start -ai debian-lab

# Open a new Bash session in a running container
docker exec -it debian-lab bash

# Execute a command inside a running container
docker exec debian-lab cat /etc/os-release
```

**Important differences:**

* `docker run` creates a new container.
* `docker start -ai` starts an existing stopped container and attaches to its main process.
* `docker exec -it` starts a separate interactive process inside a running container.

## 13. View Container Logs

```powershell
# Display container logs
docker logs debian-lab

# Follow new log entries
docker logs -f debian-lab

# Display the last 50 lines
docker logs --tail 50 debian-lab

# Display logs from the last 30 minutes
docker logs --since 30m debian-lab
```

Docker logs generally capture the main process's standard output and standard error, not every command executed through `docker exec`.

## 14. Inspect Container Configuration

```powershell
# Display detailed container configuration
docker inspect debian-lab

# Display the container's current state
docker inspect --format='{{.State.Status}}' debian-lab

# Display its exit code
docker inspect --format='{{.State.ExitCode}}' debian-lab

# Display its IP address
docker inspect --format='{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.IPAddress}}{{end}}' debian-lab
```

Inspection can reveal network settings, mounts, environment variables, runtime state, and other configuration details.

Avoid sharing inspection output without reviewing it for sensitive information.

## 15. Monitor Resource Usage

```powershell
# Monitor running containers
docker stats

# Monitor a specific container
docker stats debian-lab

# Display processes inside a container
docker top debian-lab
```

Use `docker stats` to monitor CPU, memory, network I/O, and block I/O.

## 16. Manage Images

```powershell
# List local images
docker images

# Download an image
docker pull debian:bookworm

# Inspect an image
docker image inspect debian:bookworm

# Display image build history
docker history debian:bookworm

# Remove an image
docker rmi IMAGE_ID
```

An image is a template used to create containers. A container's writable layer is separate from the image.

An image cannot normally be removed while a container still references it.

## 17. Manage Docker Networks

```powershell
# List networks
docker network ls

# Inspect a network
docker network inspect bridge

# Create a user-defined bridge network
docker network create lab-network

# Connect a running container
docker network connect lab-network debian-lab

# Disconnect a container
docker network disconnect lab-network debian-lab

# Remove a network
docker network rm lab-network
```

User-defined bridge networks support communication between connected containers and provide container-name DNS resolution.

## 18. Manage Docker Volumes

```powershell
# List volumes
docker volume ls

# Create a named volume
docker volume create lab-data

# Inspect a volume
docker volume inspect lab-data

# Remove a volume
docker volume rm lab-data
```

Mount a named volume into a container:

```powershell
docker run -it --name debian-volume --mount source=lab-data,target=/data debian:bookworm bash
```

Volumes allow data to persist independently of a container's lifecycle.

**Warning:** Removing a volume deletes its stored data. Verify that the volume is no longer needed before deleting it.

## 19. Remove Unused Docker Resources

```powershell
# Display Docker disk usage
docker system df

# Remove stopped containers
docker container prune

# Remove dangling images
docker image prune

# Remove unused networks
docker network prune

# Remove unused build cache
docker builder prune
```

Review the resources affected before confirming cleanup operations.

The following command is more aggressive:

```powershell
docker system prune -a
```

It can remove unused images in addition to other eligible resources.

Avoid adding `--volumes` unless you have reviewed the potential data loss.

## 20. Publish Container Ports

```powershell
# Run Nginx and publish container port 80 on host port 8080
docker run -d --name web-server -p 127.0.0.1:8080:80 nginx:alpine

# Display published ports
docker port web-server

# Inspect container port configuration
docker inspect --format='{{json .NetworkSettings.Ports}}' web-server
```

Open the following address on the Docker host:

`http://localhost:8080`

The mapping format is:

`HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT`

Binding to `127.0.0.1` limits the published port to the host's IPv4 loopback interface.

